  "query_preview": "..\n\n- A warm front will bring widespread light snowfall through this\n  evening.\n\n- A warming and drying trend will arrive by the middle of next\n  week.\n\nAn approaching warm front will track across the region today, \nbringing widespread light precipitation to western Montana and \nnorth central Idaho, with the heaviest amounts focused over north-\ncentral Idaho and northwest Montana. Lingering cold air will \nensure most precipitation falls as snow. Mountain passes along the\nMontana/Idaho border are expected to receive a few inches of \naccumulation, resulting in generally minor travel impacts, while \nwarm ground temperatures and daytime melting will limit \naccumulation across the lower elevations.\n\nA ridge of high pressure will build over the region from Monday \nthrough Wednesday, promoting the development of strengthening \nvalley inversions. The primary hazards during this timeframe will \nbe freezing fog and low stratus. These inversions may be strong \nenough to trap fog and low clouds in the valleys throughout the \nday, which will significantly limit daytime heating and high \ntemperatures.\n\nBy Thanksgiving, forecast guidance indicates a transition to a \nwetter pattern driven by moist southwesterly flow. This system is\nexpected to bring accumulating snow to the higher terrain, \nresulting in at least moderate travel impacts across the mountain \npasses, especially favoring the Idaho/ Western Montana border. \nFollowing a brief return of high pressure Friday into Saturday, an\nactive weather pattern is expected to resume by Sunday. Rising \nsnow levels over the weekend will likely restrict late weekend \nsnow accumulations and associated travel impacts to the highest \nmountain passes.",
